    {
      "id": "library/arxiv-0902.0350v1/blocks/prose-sec-002",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "The Flyspeck project",
      "pageStart": 3,
      "pageEnd": 5,
      "target": "../sections/sec-002-1-the-flyspeck-project.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The purpose of a long-term project, called the Flyspeck project, is to give a formal proof of the Kepler conjecture.  This section makes some preliminary remarks about\nformal proofs and gives a general overview of the current status of this project.\n\n\n\n\\subsection{Formal proof}\n\nA formal proof is a proof in which every logical inference has been checked, all the way back to the foundational axioms of mathematics.  No step is skipped no matter how obvious it may be to a mathematician.  \nA formal proof may be less intuitive, and yet is less susceptible to logical\nerrors.  Because of the large nu",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/arxiv-0902.0350v1/blocks/prose-sec-003",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Blueprint edition of the Kepler conjecture",
      "pageStart": 5,
      "pageEnd": 7,
      "target": "../sections/sec-003-2-blueprint-edition-of-the-kepler-conjecture.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "\\label{sec:blueprint}\n\nThe {\\it blueprint edition} of the proof of the Kepler conjecture is a second-generation proof that contains far more explicit detail than the original proof.  The blueprint edition is available at \\cite{hales:2008:blueprint}, \\cite{hales:2008:collection}.   Many proofs have been significantly simplified and systematized.   It has been written in a manner to permit easy formalization.  As its name might suggest, this version is intended as a blueprint for the construction of a formal proof.\nThis section \ncompares the blueprint edition with the original.\n\n\n\n\\subsection{Le",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/arxiv-0902.0350v1/blocks/prose-sec-004",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Formalizing the ordinary mathematics",
      "pageStart": 7,
      "pageEnd": 10,
      "target": "../sections/sec-004-3-formalizing-the-ordinary-mathematics.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "\\label{sec:ordinary}\n\nThis section describes some of the\nwork on formalizing Euclidean space and measure theory, and the development of\nfurther proof automation, which should be useful in this endeavor.\n\nThe computer code in Flyspeck has so far received the lion's share of the\nformal effort. This is entirely reasonable since there are, or at least were,\nreal questions about the feasibility of reproducing these results in a formal\nway. However, the Flyspeck proof includes a large amount of `ordinary'\nmathematics, which also needs to be formalized. Here we are on fairly safe\nground in principle,",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/arxiv-0902.0350v1/blocks/prose-sec-005",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Standard ML reimplementation of code",
      "pageStart": 10,
      "pageEnd": 12,
      "target": "../sections/sec-005-4-standard-ml-reimplementation-of-code.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "\\label{sec:code}\n\nThis section describes a reimplementation of the computer code \nused in the proof of the Kepler conjecture. \nThe code has been substantially\nredesigned to avoid various difficulties\nwith the original implementation.\n\n\n\\subsection{Code}\n\nThe original proof of the Kepler conjecture relies significantly on\ncomputation. Computer code is used extensively and is central both to\nthe correctness of the result and to a thorough understanding of the\nproof.\n\n  There are four major difficulties with understanding and verifying\nthe original code base. The first and most glaring difficulty",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/arxiv-0902.0350v1/blocks/prose-sec-006",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Proving nonlinear inequalities with Bernstein bases",
      "pageStart": 12,
      "pageEnd": 16,
      "target": "../sections/sec-006-5-proving-nonlinear-inequalities-with-bernstein.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "\\label{sec:zumkeller}\n\n\nThe hardest computational part of the original proof of the Kepler conjecture is the verification of a list of about a thousand nonlinear inequalities. This section presents a technique aimed at proving\nthem, based on polynomial approximation and Bernstein bases. We feel that\nthis approach better fits the requirements of formal proof, as outlined in\nSection~\\ref{sec:sean}.  We hope to refine the method to cover all Flyspeck\ninequalities.\n\nWe exhibit the method on a single inequality~\\calc{586468779}.\nThe original proof contains the following definitions \\cite{sp1}:\n\\beg",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/arxiv-0902.0350v1/blocks/prose-sec-007",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Tame graph enumeration",
      "pageStart": 16,
      "pageEnd": 19,
      "target": "../sections/sec-007-6-tame-graph-enumeration.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "\\label{sec:graph}\n\nTame graphs are particular plane graphs that represent potential\ncounterexamples to the Kepler conjecture. The \\emph{Archive} is a list of over 5000 plane\ngraphs.  The original proof generates the Archive with the help of a Java program that\nenumerates all plane graphs. Tameness is defined\nin Section~18 and the enumeration is sketched in Section~19 of\n\\cite{Hales:2006:DCG}.\nThis section sketches the formally machine-checked proof of Claim~3.13 and\nTheorem 19.1 in the original proof~\\cite{Hales:2006:DCG}:\n\\begin{thm}\\label{Archive:complete}\nAny tame plane graph is isomorphic",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/arxiv-0902.0350v1/blocks/prose-sec-008",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Verifying linear programs",
      "pageStart": 19,
      "pageEnd": 22,
      "target": "../sections/sec-008-7-verifying-linear-programs.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "\\label{sec:lp}\n\nThis section reports on the current state of the formal verification of\nthe linear programming part of the proof of the Kepler conjecture.\nThe results of the linear programming in the original proof are recorded\nin several gigabytes of log files.\nThis section presents the formalization of the generation and bounding of these linear programs \nin the mechanical proof assistant Isabelle~\\cite{LNCS2283}. \nA more detailed version of the material presented here can be found in S. Obua's thesis~\\cite{obua:phd}.\n\nThis formalization relies on the archive of tame graphs. Each tame graph",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
